    Celery
6. Django application structure

Your project contains separate Django apps because each app represents a business responsibility.

wholsaler_erp/
│
├── accounts/
├── products/
├── orders/
├── billing/
├── payments/
├── customers/
├── shipping/
│
├── templates/
├── static/
├── media/
│
├── wholsaler_erp/
│
└── manage.py

The important principle is:

One app should have a clear responsibility.

For example:

accounts → users and profiles

products → products and inventory

orders → ordering workflow

billing → invoices

payments → payments and ledger

customers → retailer's customers

shipping → delivery


This makes the project easier to maintain and scale.

PHASE 1 — Project Setup
Goal

Create a clean Django project with the correct foundation.

You learn:

Django project
Django apps
settings
URLs
templates
static files
media files
database
virtual environment
configuration
Project vs App

This is an important interview question.

Project

The project is the overall Django application configuration.

Example:

wholsaler_erp/

It contains:

settings.py
urls.py
asgi.py
wsgi.py
App

An app handles a particular business function.

Example:

products/
orders/
billing/

Interview answer:

A Django project is the complete application configuration, while a Django app is a modular component that handles a specific business functionality.

PHASE 2 — Authentication & Profiles

Now we need users.

Django already provides:

User

But our business needs additional information.

Therefore:

User
 │
 ├── UserProfile
 │
 ├── WholesalerProfile
 │
 └── RetailerProfile
Why not store everything inside User?

Django's User contains things such as:

username
email
password
first_name
last_name

But wholesaler needs:

business_name
GST number
phone
address
city
state
pincode

Retailer needs:

shop_name
owner_name
GST
phone
address
city
state
pincode

Therefore separate profile models are appropriate.

UserProfile

It determines:

WHOLESALER

or

RETAILER

Conceptually:

User
 ↓
UserProfile
 ↓
Role

The role controls what the user is allowed to do.

Authentication vs Authorization

Very important interview topic.

Authentication

Who are you?

Example:

username + password
Authorization

What are you allowed to do?

Example:

Retailer cannot access wholesaler orders.
PHASE 3 — Wholesaler ↔ Retailer Relationship

This is a major database-design concept.

One wholesaler can have:

Retailer A
Retailer B
Retailer C

One retailer can work with:

Wholesaler X
Wholesaler Y
Wholesaler Z

Therefore:

Many-to-Many

relationship.

Database concept:

Wholesaler
      │
      │
      ▼
 Junction Table
      ▲
      │
      │
Retailer

Django handles the intermediate table through:

ManyToManyField
Why Many-to-Many?

Because:

1 wholesaler → many retailers
1 retailer → many wholesalers

If you incorrectly use ForeignKey from retailer to wholesaler, one retailer could belong to only one wholesaler.

That would not match the real business requirement.

PHASE 4 — Product Management

Now the wholesaler can create products.

A product belongs to a wholesaler.

Relationship:

Wholesaler
     │
     ├── Product
     ├── Product
     └── Product

Example:

Product
----------------
Name: Hing
SKU: HING0001
Category: Grocery
Unit: Kg
Description: Asafoetida
Why SKU?

SKU means:

Stock Keeping Unit.

It uniquely identifies a product within the business.

Example:

HING0001
RICE0001
SUGAR0001

It is useful for:

searching
inventory
billing
reports
APIs
barcode integration later
Product vs Stock

This distinction is extremely important.

Product

Describes what the item is.

Hing
SKU
Category
Unit
Description
Stock

Describes how much you have and at what price.

Quantity
Purchase price
Selling price
MRP
Batch
Expiry
Minimum stock

Therefore:

Product
   │
   └── Stock
         │
         ├── quantity
         ├── price
         ├── batch
         └── expiry

This is a very good interview design discussion.

PHASE 5 — Stock Management

Now we manage inventory.

Example:

Product = Hing

Stock:
Quantity = 500
Purchase Price = ₹160
Selling Price = ₹190
MRP = ₹220

Stock also contains:

batch_number
expiry_date
minimum_stock
Why batch number?

Suppose:

Batch A → 100 units → expires January
Batch B → 200 units → expires March

You need to know which stock was sold first.

That becomes important for expiry management.

Why minimum_stock?

Suppose:

Current stock = 20
Minimum stock = 50

Then:

20 <= 50

So the ERP identifies:

Low Stock.

This allows the business to reorder before stock runs out.

PHASE 6 — Low Stock Detection

The system automatically finds stock where:

quantity <= minimum_stock

Example:

Product       Quantity    Minimum
---------------------------------
Hing             20         50
Rice              8         20
Sugar            100         50

Result:

Hing → Low
Rice → Low
Sugar → Normal

This is not just a UI feature.

It demonstrates:

database filtering
F() expressions
business rules
query optimization
PHASE 7 — Expiry Management

Some products expire.

We classify stock as:

Expired
Expiring Soon
Good
No Expiry

For example:

Today = 8 Oct

Expiry = 5 Oct
→ EXPIRED

Expiry = 20 Oct
→ EXPIRING SOON

Expiry = 15 Dec
→ GOOD

The system should prevent expired stock from being transferred.

This becomes important during fulfillment.

PHASE 8 — Search & Filters

A real ERP cannot expect users to scroll through thousands of products.

Therefore we support:

Search

By:

Product name
SKU
Filter

By:

Category
Stock status
Expiry status

Example:

Search:
hing

returns:

Hing
Hing Powder
Hing Premium

This teaches:

icontains
Q()
F()

and query composition.

PHASE 9 — Order Creation

Now we enter the most important business workflow.

Retailer selects:

Wholesaler
    ↓
Product
    ↓
Quantity
    ↓
Notes

Example:

Retailer:
Sayam Medical

Wholesaler:
ABC Wholesale

Product:
Hing

Quantity:
220

The system creates:

Order
OrderItem
Order vs OrderItem

One order can contain multiple products.

Example:

Order #10

Hing       220
Sugar       50
Rice        30

Therefore:

Order
 │
 ├── OrderItem
 ├── OrderItem
 └── OrderItem

This is:

One-to-Many
Why not put product directly inside Order?

Because one order can contain multiple products.

Therefore:

Order → OrderItems

is the correct normalized design.

Order Price Snapshot

When an order is created, we store:

price_per_unit
total_price

inside OrderItem.

Why?

Because prices can change later.

Suppose:

Today:
Hing = ₹190

Customer orders 100.

Later:

Hing = ₹210

The original order should still represent:

₹190 × 100

This is called preserving a historical price snapshot.

Very important in financial systems.

PHASE 10 — Retailer Order Management

Retailer can see:

My Orders

with:

Order number
Date
Wholesaler
Status
Amount

They can open an order to see:

Products
Quantity
Price
Total
Status
Notes
Object-Level Authorization

This is one of the most important security concepts in your project.

Suppose:

Retailer A

tries to access:

Order #500

belonging to:

Retailer B

The application must reject it.

Therefore we query:

Order.objects.get(
    id=order_id,
    retailer=retailer
)

instead of:

Order.objects.get(id=order_id)

This prevents an IDOR-style authorization vulnerability.

PHASE 11 — Wholesaler Order Management

Wholesaler receives orders.

Initial status:

PENDING

Wholesaler can:

ACCEPT

or:

REJECT
PHASE 12 — Stock Transfer

After acceptance, stock becomes involved.

The important business rule is:

Placing an order does NOT immediately deduct wholesaler stock.

Why?

Because the wholesaler hasn't accepted/fulfilled it yet.

So:

Order Created
      ↓
PENDING
      ↓
Wholesaler Accepts
      ↓
Check Stock

Only after fulfillment:

Wholesaler Stock
       ↓
     Deduct
       ↓
Retailer Stock
       ↑
     Add
Why transaction.atomic?

Imagine:

Wholesaler stock deducted

but then:

Retailer stock update fails

You would have:

Wholesaler: -220
Retailer:   +0

That is data corruption.

With:

transaction.atomic()

the operation becomes:

BEGIN
   ↓
Deduct stock
   ↓
Add retailer stock
   ↓
Update order
   ↓
COMMIT

If anything fails:

ROLLBACK

Everything returns to its previous state.

This is a major backend engineering concept.

PHASE 13 — Waiting for Stock

Suppose:

Requested = 220
Available = 100

The order cannot be fulfilled.

But we don't necessarily want to reject it.

Instead:

WAITING_FOR_STOCK

This means:

The wholesaler accepted the business request, but currently doesn't have sufficient inventory to fulfill it.

Later:

New stock arrives
       ↓
Stock = 300
       ↓
Fulfill waiting order

This represents a real-world inventory workflow.

PHASE 14 — Fulfill Waiting Orders

When stock becomes available, wholesaler can click:

Fulfill Order

The service checks again.

It must verify:

Order status
Stock quantity
Expiry
Product
Retailer
FIFO / FEFO concept

Your implementation orders stock by:

expiry_date
created_at

This is similar to FEFO:

First Expired, First Out.

For products with expiry dates, stock expiring sooner should generally be consumed first.

Example:

Batch A → expires Oct 15
Batch B → expires Nov 20

Fulfill from:

Batch A first

This reduces wastage.

PHASE 15 — Invoice & Billing

After actual fulfillment:

Order
  ↓
Invoice

Invoice contains:

Invoice number
Order
Wholesaler
Retailer
Invoice date
Subtotal
Discount
Tax
Grand total
Status
Invoice Item

An invoice can contain multiple products:

Invoice
 │
 ├── InvoiceItem
 ├── InvoiceItem
 └── InvoiceItem

Each item contains:

Product
Quantity
Price
Tax rate
Tax amount
Total
Why Invoice is separate from Order?

Because an order represents:

What the customer requested.

An invoice represents:

What is financially owed.

They are related but have different business meanings.

For example:

Order:
220 units requested

Invoice:
220 units fulfilled
₹41,800 payable
Invoice Status

Your system supports:

UNPAID
PARTIALLY_PAID
PAID
CANCELLED

Example:

Invoice = ₹41,800

No payment
→ UNPAID

₹10,000 paid
→ PARTIALLY_PAID

₹31,800 more
→ PAID
GST / Tax

Your invoice calculates item-level tax.

Example:

Product amount = ₹10,000
GST = 5%

Tax = ₹500

Total = ₹10,500

The system sums all item tax amounts.

Invoice Number

Instead of exposing only database ID:

1

we generate:

INV-2026-000001

This is more appropriate for a business application.

PHASE 16 — Payment Management

After invoice generation, retailer can make payments.

Payment contains:

Invoice
Amount
Payment Method
Reference Number
Notes

Payment methods could include:

Cash
UPI
Bank Transfer
Cheque
Card
Partial Payments

Suppose:

Invoice = ₹41,800

Payment:

₹10,000

Then:

Paid = ₹10,000
Outstanding = ₹31,800

Invoice:

PARTIALLY_PAID

Then another:

₹31,800

Now:

Paid = ₹41,800
Outstanding = ₹0

Invoice:

PAID
Overpayment Protection

The service checks:

payment <= outstanding

So:

Outstanding = ₹31,800
Payment = ₹40,000

is rejected.

This is important because financial systems should never silently accept invalid payments.

Transaction Safety

Payment creation performs several operations:

Create Payment
      ↓
Create Ledger Entry
      ↓
Update Invoice Status

These should succeed together.

Therefore:

@transaction.atomic

is important.

If ledger creation fails:

Payment should also rollback.
PHASE 17 — Outstanding / Credit Ledger

This is where your ERP becomes much more like a real financial system.

Think of the ledger as:

A chronological financial record between a wholesaler and retailer.

Debit

When wholesaler generates an invoice:

Retailer owes money

Therefore:

DEBIT

Example:

Invoice = ₹41,800

Ledger:
DEBIT ₹41,800
Credit

When retailer makes a payment:

Retailer's liability decreases.

Therefore:

CREDIT

Example:

Payment = ₹10,000

Ledger:
CREDIT ₹10,000
Outstanding calculation

The system calculates:

Outstanding
=
Total Debit - Total Credit

Example:

Debit:
₹41,800

Credit:
₹10,000

Outstanding:
₹31,800
Multiple invoices

Suppose retailer has:

Invoice 1 = ₹40,000
Invoice 2 = ₹20,000
Invoice 3 = ₹15,000

Total debit:

₹75,000

Payments:

₹20,000

Outstanding:

₹55,000

This allows the wholesaler to understand:

How much money this retailer currently owes the business.

Why LedgerEntry instead of calculating only from Invoice?

Because a ledger provides a financial history.

Example:

Date        Type       Amount
--------------------------------
Oct 1       DEBIT      ₹40,000
Oct 3       CREDIT     ₹10,000
Oct 7       DEBIT      ₹20,000
Oct 8       CREDIT     ₹15,000

Now the business can audit the account.

Phase 18 — Retailer Customer Management

Now we move to the retailer's side.

Remember:

Wholesaler
     ↓
Retailer
     ↓
Retailer's Customers

Example:

Retailer:
Sayam Medical Store

Customers:
Rahul
Amit
Priya

The retailer can maintain:

Customer name
Phone
Email
Address
GST
Why separate customers from retailers?

Because there are two different business relationships.

Wholesaler → Retailer

is B2B.

But:

Retailer → Customer

is the retailer's downstream business.

The ERP therefore supports a chain:

Wholesaler
    ↓
Retailer
    ↓
Customer
PHASE 19 — Customer Invoices

Retailer sells products to customers.

Therefore retailer generates:

Customer Invoice

Example:

Customer:
Rahul

Product:
Hing

Quantity:
2

Price:
₹220

Total:
₹440

This invoice is different from the wholesaler invoice.

Two financial levels

Your ERP eventually handles:

Level 1
Wholesaler → Retailer
Level 2
Retailer → Customer

This is an important architectural feature.

PHASE 20 — Customer Payments

Customer may pay:

Full amount

or:

Partial amount

Example:

Customer invoice = ₹5,000
Paid = ₹2,000
Outstanding = ₹3,000

Customer payment records therefore need similar business logic to supplier/wholesaler payments.

PHASE 21 — Shipping & Delivery Tracking

After order processing:

PROCESSING
    ↓
SHIPPED
    ↓
DELIVERED

Shipping information can contain:

Courier
Tracking number
Shipping date
Expected delivery
Delivered date
Delivery status
Why shipping should be separate from order?

Because:

Order = business transaction
Shipping = logistics transaction

One order may eventually have more complex delivery behavior.

Keeping shipping separate improves maintainability.

