PHASE 26 — Security & Permissions

This phase is extremely important for job interviews.

You need to understand:

CSRF

Protects against forged state-changing requests.

Django provides CSRF protection.

XSS

Cross-Site Scripting.

Never blindly render user-controlled HTML.

SQL Injection

Don't construct SQL using unsafe string concatenation.

Django ORM protects against many common SQL injection scenarios when used correctly.

Password security

Passwords should never be stored as plain text.

Django hashes passwords.

Authorization

A user must only access their own business data.

For example:

Retailer A

must not see:

Retailer B's invoices.
Security principle

Never trust:

URL ID
POST data
hidden input
query parameter

Just because the browser sends:

invoice_id = 10

doesn't mean the user owns invoice 10.

Always verify ownership server-side.

PHASE 27 — PostgreSQL & Database Optimization

During development:

SQLite

is convenient.

For production:

PostgreSQL

is much more suitable for this ERP.

You will learn:

Indexes
Transactions
Constraints
Foreign Keys
Query optimization
Locks
Isolation
Why indexes?

Suppose you have:

5,000,000 orders

and search:

invoice_number

Without appropriate indexing, database searches can become expensive.

Indexes help databases find records efficiently.

N+1 Query Problem

Suppose:

orders = Order.objects.all()

and template accesses:

order.retailer.shop_name

for every order.

You may accidentally produce:

1 query for orders
+
100 queries for retailers

= 101 queries.

Instead:

select_related("retailer")

can fetch related foreign-key data efficiently.

select_related vs prefetch_related

Very important interview question.

select_related

Generally used for:

ForeignKey
OneToOne

It uses SQL joins.

prefetch_related

Used for:

ManyToMany
Reverse ForeignKey

It performs separate queries and joins results in Python.

